              "text": "You **cannot legally buy a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel (*Pteromys momonga*)** as a pet. \n\nWhile they are incredibly popular on social media due to their big eyes and round, anime-like appearance, several strict legal and environmental barriers prevent them from being kept as pets:\n\n### Why You Can't Buy One\n* **Strict Japanese Export Laws:** The Japanese dwarf flying squirrel is endemic to Japan (specifically the islands of Honshu and Kyushu). Japan has highly restrictive wildlife conservation laws that strictly prohibit the capture, domestic commercial sale, and international export of native wild species.\n* **No Captive Breeding Programs:** Because they cannot be legally exported, there are no USDA-licensed breeders or legitimate domestic breeding colonies of *Pteromys momonga* in North America, Europe, or other regions outside of Japan.\n* **Import Bans on Rodents:** In many countries, including the United States, there are strict quarantine laws and permanent bans on importing wild-caught rodents to prevent the spread of diseases (such as monkeypox).\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Warning About Online Scams\nBecause of their high demand, many fraudulent websites and online \"exotic pet shops\" claim to have Japanese dwarf flying squirrels for sale. **These are 100% scams.** They will typically ask for payment via untraceable methods (like Zelle, CashApp, or cryptocurrency) and then demand further \"shipping insurance\" or \"quarantine fees\" without ever sending an animal. \n\n### Legal and Realistic Alternatives\nIf you are looking for a highly adorable, gliding \"pocket pet,\" there are several legal and accessible options:\n\n1. **The Southern Flying Squirrel (*Glaucomys volans*):** \n   * **What they are:** These are native to North America and are very similar in size, behavior, and care to their Japanese cousins. \n   * **How to buy:** They are widely bred in captivity by licensed exotic animal breeders in the United States. \n   * **Legality:** Keeping them is legal in many U.S. states, though some states require a permit, and others ban them entirely. Always check your state and local laws regarding native wildlife before purchasing.\n2. **Sugar Gliders (*Petaurus breviceps*):** \n   * **What they are:** Although they are marsupials rather than rodents, they look very similar, have gliding membranes, and are highly social.\n   * **How to buy:** They are incredibly common in the pet trade and can easily be purchased from reputable breeders or adopted from shelters.\n3. **Plushies and Merchandise:** \n   * If you specifically love the look of the Japanese dwarf flying squirrel, high-quality plush toys (such as those by Wild Republic), 3D-printed articulated figurines, and other merchandise are widely available online.\n\n### Seeing Them in the Wild\nIf you want to experience these animals in real life, the best way is to travel to Japan. Guided night tours in forested areas like **Mount Takao** (near Tokyo) or looking for their cousin, the *Ezo momonga* (Siberian flying squirrel), on the northern island of **Hokkaido**, are highly popular options for wildlife enthusiasts.",
